  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/31-crayfishes-shrimps-crabs-snails-mussels/colorful_great_ramshorn_snails_en/",
      "html": "<p>The Great Ramshorn snail (Planorbarius corneus, formerly known as Planorbis corneus) has been kept in aquaria and garden ponds since the beginning of the hobby. It grows to a relatively large size (maximum 4 cm) and does not touch living plants. This snail has red blood that contains hemoglobin, so albinos have a bright red body.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "waterConditions",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/31-crayfishes-shrimps-crabs-snails-mussels/colorful_great_ramshorn_snails_en/",
      "html": "<p>The water temperature should be between 12 and 25°C, hardness should be at least 10° KH (more is better), and the pH can be between 6.5 and 8.5.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/31-crayfishes-shrimps-crabs-snails-mussels/colorful_great_ramshorn_snails_en/",
      "html": "<p>They feed exclusively on algae and detritus.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/31-crayfishes-shrimps-crabs-snails-mussels/colorful_great_ramshorn_snails_en/",
      "html": "<p>They breathe through lungs, so at least part of the oxygen they need is taken from the air. The maximum age they can reach is about 3 years, but in the wild very few specimens get that old.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/31-crayfishes-shrimps-crabs-snails-mussels/colorful_great_ramshorn_snails_en/",
      "html": "<p>Keeping and breeding the Great Ramshorn is extremely easy. As they are hermaphrodites, any two animals are a pair. Breeders in Singapore have succeeded in breeding full red and blue sports of this snail.</p>"
    }
  ],
